This is useful for immutable position data because the reward rate and reward amount are stored on the position at creation time.
That behavior is consistent with the new immutable-term principle.
However, the table currently does not contain a direct term_id / term_version_id. This makes historical contract provenance weaker than the new framework requires.
Future direction: a new stake position should reference the exact published term/version used to create it, while retaining the copied contractual values needed for settlement/audit.
Do not add this field by rewriting existing stakes until a migration plan is approved.
9. Current Withdrawal Contract
There are two overloaded create_my_withdrawal_request(...) functions.
Both are Mainnet-only.
The current withdrawal logic:
allows reward or capital
requires positive amount
requires matured stake (unlock_at <= now())
blocks if another request for the same stake/type is pending or approved
applies a fixed 1% fee
checks requested amount plus fee against stakes.reward_amount or stakes.amount
stores the request as pending
The API also uses a fixed minimum withdrawal of 1 Pi.
Important contract gaps relative to Framework v1
The current implementation does not yet provide the complete project-specific policy layer for:
maturity-only vs early withdrawal
partial withdrawal rules
reward forfeiture/recalculation
project-specific withdrawal consequences
term-version-driven withdrawal policy
liquidity-aware withdrawal eligibility
explicit contract snapshot/version linkage
It also means the current 1% fee is a legacy implementation detail, not a universal ALBUKHR market rule.
No fee migration is authorized by this audit.
10. Current Withdrawal Settlement
The API payout executor receives only the withdrawal request ID and reads the authoritative amount, UID and wallet from the database. It protects against duplicate in-process payout execution and recovers incomplete A2U payments.
It requires the withdrawal request to be approved, validates the referenced stake and project context, completes the Pi A2U payout, and only then sets the withdrawal request to paid.
This settlement ordering is compatible with the new fail-safe principle:
Do not mark a payout successful before the underlying Pi payment is confirmed.
However, the current executor is not yet a full treasury/liquidity-aware settlement engine. That work belongs to the implementation phase after the Liquidity and Staking frameworks are converted to authoritative data contracts.
11. Current Liquidity Settlement Contract
public.settle_project_liquidity_payment(...) currently:
Requires a valid Mainnet Core project.
Requires project status approved or active.
Requires an active/locked Mainnet treasury.
Verifies the recipient against the treasury wallet.
Inserts a verified liquidity payment.
Adds the payment amount to project_treasury.verified_liquidity.
Writes a treasury transaction.
If the project is approved, required liquidity is met, and an exact active project-code term exists, it can promote the project to active.
This is compatible with the hardened Core activation gate, but it remains a specialized Core liquidity contract.
The new Market Framework should preserve the safety invariant while eventually separating:
treasury readiness
liquidity readiness
reward-capacity readiness
activation authorization
rather than allowing a single liquidity payment event to silently perform every market-readiness decision for every project type.
