This does not authorize deletion or rewriting of the existing term records; they remain configuration that should be retained until the migration strategy is approved.
13. Contract Alignment Decision
The preferred target model is:
Project (projects.id) → Published Staking Term → Term Version → User Position
with project_code retained as a human/registry identifier, but not treated as the sole foreign-key-like contract identity.
Target conceptual structure:
projects.id
   ↓
project_staking_terms.project_id
   ↓
project_staking_term_versions.id
   ↓
stakes.term_version_id
The final names may differ after schema audit, but the relationship should be explicit.
For an existing project position, the position must preserve the exact contractual parameters that were accepted at creation.
14. What Must Not Be Done Yet
Do not yet:
rename AZMAN terms to AZMAN-4080
rename LABBAIKA terms to LABBAIKA-3980
rename BARSH terms to BARSH-1125
rename URBAN terms to URBAN-5180
rename KHAIRAT terms to KHAIRAT-3132
rename HAUWAL terms to HAUWAL-6552
change reward rates
change durations
change the current 1% withdrawal fee
activate a project by direct status update
generalize Core payment routes to Internal/External by removing the Core restriction
delete legacy term rows
Those are implementation actions that require the target contract to be approved first.
15. Required Next Technical Migration
The next implementation should be a Staking Contract Schema v2 design, not a reward-rate migration.
It should establish:
A. Project-specific identity
Each staking term belongs to a specific projects.id and network.
B. Term versions
A term can be superseded without changing existing positions.
C. Contract snapshot
Each stake records the exact term/version and the immutable contractual values required for settlement.
D. Capability-specific eligibility
Investment eligibility should resolve from the project's activation scope and the exact published term, not from a frontend duration list.
E. Withdrawal policy
Withdrawal eligibility must resolve from the position's contract, not from a global fixed rule.
F. Project-type boundary
Core, Internal and External project investment routes must remain distinct unless the authoritative market contract explicitly defines a shared interface.
16. Safety Assessment
No production financial records were changed during this audit.
The current strongest safety properties are:
Mainnet-only financial route enforcement
exact project/network validation in the Core investment contract
server-side payment verification
idempotent stake creation by payment record
stake/payment uniqueness
withdrawal request deduplication for the same stake/type
payout completion before marking withdrawal paid
fail-closed Core activation checks
The main architectural risks are:
legacy term identity mismatch
frontend hard-coded term/duration assumptions
no term-version reference from stake positions
withdrawal policy not yet project/term driven
treasury/liquidity controls not yet represented as a complete market-level capability contract
17. Recommended Implementation Sequence
Approve this technical contract-alignment decision.
Design Staking Contract Schema v2.
Add term/project/version relationships without touching existing positions.
Add authoritative server-side term-read RPCs.
Update Core payment/staking creation to consume the new term contract.
Add Internal/External contract boundaries separately.
Add withdrawal-policy resolution.
Update frontend staking forms to consume authoritative terms rather than hard-coded terms.
Run Testnet migration and regression tests.
Migrate/republish existing configuration into the new model only after tests pass.
Activate projects only through the readiness/activation standard.
18. Final Audit Conclusion
The current ALBUKHR financial system has a workable safety foundation, but its legacy staking-term identity is not yet aligned with the new Project Market Framework.
The central issue is not the current reward percentages themselves. The central issue is contract identity and ownership of the staking terms.
The target should therefore be:
Project-specific + versioned + server-authoritative + immutable-per-position + liquidity/risk/disclosure aware.
This audit authorizes no production data mutation. The next work item is the Staking Contract Schema v2 design and compatibility migration plan.
